<div class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">Leadership &amp; People Development Academy Â· Module 3</div><h1>Supervisor &amp; Team Leader Development</h1><p class="lead">Move from being a strong individual contributor to leading a team reliably. Practise the daily habits that help people understand priorities, perform to standards, solve problems and feel accountable without being micromanaged.</p><p class="muted">Built around frontline hospitality, food and beverage, guest service, facilities and other shift-based teams.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#briefing-planner">Build a shift briefing</a><a class="btn" href="#knowledge-check">Take the knowledge check</a><a class="btn" href="leadership-people-development-academy.html">Back to Academy</a></div></div>
<section id="outcomes"><h2>1. Learning outcomes</h2><p>By the end of this module, you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain the difference between doing the work yourself and leading others to deliver it.</li><li>Run a focused pre-shift briefing with clear priorities, roles, risks and standards.</li><li>Delegate a task with an outcome, authority, resources, deadline and check-in.</li><li>Give specific, respectful feedback and coach an employee through a performance gap.</li><li>Address conflict and underperformance fairly, using evidence and workplace policy.</li><li>Monitor results and follow through on commitments without creating a blame culture.</li></ul></section>
<section id="role"><h2>2. The supervisor's role: standards, people and delivery</h2><p>A supervisor translates plans and policies into consistent work on the floor. The job is not simply to be the fastest or most experienced person on the team. It is to make the required standard clear, organise the work, support the team, notice problems early and ensure issues are resolved or escalated appropriately.</p><div class="grid"><div class="tile"><h3>Set direction</h3><p>Clarify the shift's goals, guest or customer needs, service standards, safety risks and the most important priorities.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>Organise work</h3><p>Match tasks to capability and workload, confirm resources, coordinate handovers and adjust when demand changes.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>Support people</h3><p>Listen, demonstrate, coach, recognise good work and address behaviour consistently and respectfully.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>Own follow-through</h3><p>Check results, record relevant facts, close open actions and escalate issues outside your authority.</p></div></div><h3>Moving from peer to leader</h3><p>Promotion can change relationships with former colleagues. Be approachable without favouritism, apply the same standards to friends and others, protect confidential information and explain decisions where appropriate. Do not use rank to win arguments; use clear expectations, evidence, policy and calm judgement.</p><p><strong>Daily test:</strong> does the team know what matters, who owns each task, what good looks like and when to ask for help?</p></section>
<section id="briefings"><h2>3. Run a purposeful pre-shift briefing</h2><p>A good briefing is short enough to remember and specific enough to act on. It should not become a long speech or a list of vague instructions. Adapt it to the operation and include safety-critical information required by your workplace procedures.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Briefing element</th><th>Supervisor action</th><th>Check for understanding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expected demand</td><td>Share reservations, events, arrivals, busy periods or workload forecast.</td><td>Ask which period or task needs extra preparation.</td></tr><tr><td>Team deployment</td><td>Name roles, sections, task owners and support arrangements.</td><td>Ask each person to confirm their assignment.</td></tr><tr><td>Standards and risks</td><td>Highlight relevant safety, quality, hygiene, allergy, security or guest-care points.</td><td>Ask a team member to repeat the critical control or escalation route.</td></tr><tr><td>Service priorities</td><td>State the key service goal and any known guest needs without disclosing unnecessary private details.</td><td>Ask what the team should prioritise if demand peaks.</td></tr><tr><td>Escalation and close</td><td>Explain who to contact, where help is available and when the next check-in happens.</td><td>Invite one question and confirm readiness.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For a fast-moving operation, use a simple pattern: <strong>Purpose â†’ People â†’ Priorities â†’ Precautions â†’ Questions.</strong> Keep a record when policy requires it, especially for safety-critical updates or formal handovers.</p></section>
<section id="briefing-planner"><h2>4. Interactive shift briefing planner</h2><p>Use this worksheet to create a practical briefing outline. Enter operational information only; do not include sensitive personal or guest data.</p><label for="shift">Shift or operation</label><input id="shift" placeholder="Example: Friday dinner service"><label for="demand">Expected demand or key event</label><input id="demand" placeholder="Example: 160 covers, one private function"><label for="priority">Top service or delivery priority</label><input id="priority" placeholder="Example: accurate order communication during peak"><label for="roles">Roles and task owners</label><textarea id="roles" rows="3" placeholder="Example: Supervisorâ€”floor coordination; section owners; pass coordinator"></textarea><label for="risk">Safety, quality or escalation reminder</label><textarea id="risk" rows="3" placeholder="Example: follow approved allergy process; escalate uncertainty before service"></textarea><label for="checkin">Check-in time or trigger</label><input id="checkin" placeholder="Example: after first peak, or at 19:30"><button type="button" id="make-briefing">Generate briefing outline</button><button type="button" class="secondary" id="select-briefing">Select outline text</button><div class="output" id="briefing-output" aria-live="polite">Your briefing outline will appear here.</div><p class="muted">Review the outline before use. Follow your organisation's current policies and required briefing records.</p></section>
<section id="delegation"><h2>5. Delegate outcomes, not just activities</h2><p>Delegation is not simply telling someone to â€œtake care of it.â€ The employee needs a clear result, the boundaries of their authority, sufficient resources and a realistic deadline. The supervisor remains responsible for appropriate oversight and must not delegate decisions the employee is not authorised or competent to make.</p><div class="grid"><div class="tile"><h3>1. Define the result</h3><p>Describe what must be completed and the standard it must meet. Avoid instructions that leave success open to interpretation.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>2. Confirm ownership</h3><p>Name one accountable owner, identify collaborators and confirm the person understands the task.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>3. Provide authority</h3><p>Explain what decisions they may make, what resources are available and what requires approval.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>4. Agree a check-in</h3><p>Set a proportionate progress check and a completion time. High-risk tasks need closer verification than routine work.</p></div></div><h3>Example: preparing a service area</h3><p><strong>Vague:</strong> â€œGet the restaurant ready.â€</p><p><strong>Clearer:</strong> â€œPlease complete your section setup by 5:30 p.m. using the opening checklist. Check table settings, menus and approved service supplies; tell me immediately if anything is missing or unsafe. We'll review the checklist together at 5:25 p.m.â€</p><p>After delegation, avoid taking the task back at the first sign of difficulty. Ask what has been done, what is blocking progress and what support is needed. Intervene promptly where safety, quality or service recovery requires it.</p></section>
<section id="coaching"><h2>6. Coach for improvement and confidence</h2><p>Coaching helps a person think through a task, recognise a gap and practise a better approach. It is different from simply giving an instruction. For a new or safety-critical task, demonstrate the approved method and verify competence first; coaching does not replace mandatory training or formal controls.</p><ol><li><strong>Agree the focus:</strong> explain the specific task or behaviour to improve.</li><li><strong>Ask and listen:</strong> â€œHow do you think that went?â€ or â€œWhat made this difficult?â€</li><li><strong>Explore the gap:</strong> compare observed behaviour with the agreed standard and check for obstacles.</li><li><strong>Choose an action:</strong> agree one practical step, demonstration or practice opportunity.</li><li><strong>Follow up:</strong> set a time to observe progress and recognise improvement.</li></ol><p>Useful questions include: â€œWhat was your intended result?â€, â€œWhich part was hardest?â€, â€œWhat options do you have?â€, â€œWhat support would help?â€ and â€œHow will we know the change worked?â€ Ask questions genuinely; do not use them as disguised criticism.</p></section>
<section id="feedback"><h2>7. Give feedback that people can act on</h2><p>Effective feedback is timely, specific, based on observable behaviour and connected to a clear standard. Discuss sensitive or corrective feedback privately where possible. Recognise good work close to the event and explain exactly what should be repeated.</p><div class="grid"><div class="tile"><h3>Describe</h3><p>State what you observed, when it happened and the relevant standard. Separate facts from assumptions about intention.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>Explain impact</h3><p>Describe the effect on safety, service, quality, colleagues or workflow without exaggeration.</p></div><div class="tile"><h3>Agree next action</h3><p>State or discuss the expected behaviour, ask for the employee's view and agree support and follow-up.</p></div></div><h3>Example</h3><p>â€œDuring the last two handovers, the outstanding maintenance issue was not recorded. The next shift did not know the equipment was unavailable, which delayed preparation. From now on, please record unresolved issues in the handover log and tell the incoming supervisor directly. Is there anything preventing you from doing that?â€</p><p>Avoid labels such as â€œlazy,â€ â€œcarelessâ€ or â€œnot leadership material.â€ They judge a person rather than identifying a behaviour that can be addressed. If the issue is serious or repeated, follow the formal performance or disciplinary process and document facts according to policy.</p></section>
<section id="conflict"><h2>8. Handle conflict and difficult conversations</h2><p>Conflict may arise from unclear roles, workload, communication style, inconsistent standards or competing priorities. Deal with it early when safe to do so. Do not promise absolute confidentiality if a concern must be escalated under policy or law.</p><ol><li><strong>Pause and stabilise:</strong> lower the temperature, separate people if needed and protect guests, staff and operations from immediate risk.</li><li><strong>Hear the accounts:</strong> speak respectfully, ask each person for facts and avoid deciding the outcome based on rumour.</li><li><strong>Define the issue:</strong> identify the behaviour, operational impact, relevant standard and points of agreement.</li><li><strong>Agree the next step:</strong> clarify what must change, who will do it and when it will be reviewed.</li><li><strong>Escalate appropriately:</strong> use HR, senior management, safety or formal grievance channels for serious allegations, threats, harassment, discrimination or issues beyond your authority.</li></ol><p>Do not force mediation where there is a safety threat, serious misconduct or a power imbalance that makes it inappropriate. Follow your organisation's policies and applicable legal requirements.</p><h3>Scenario: disagreement during peak service</h3><p>Two employees argue about who should support a delayed section while guests are waiting. First assign immediate coverage and restore service. Then establish the facts, clarify role expectations and review workload allocation after the peak. Address disrespectful conduct separately and consistently; do not attempt a lengthy debate in front of guests.</p></section>
<section id="performance"><h2>9. Monitor performance fairly and consistently</h2><p>Performance management works best when expectations are clear before a problem occurs. Choose measures that the team can influence and that reflect both results and required behaviours. Avoid rewarding speed in a way that encourages unsafe shortcuts or poor service.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Possible evidence</th><th>Supervisor response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Service and quality</td><td>Observed standards, rework, complaints and positive feedback</td><td>Coach the behaviour and investigate recurring process issues.</td></tr><tr><td>Safety and compliance</td><td>Required checks, reported hazards, verified procedure use</td><td>Act immediately on risk; follow reporting and escalation rules.</td></tr><tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Task completion, punctual handovers and attendance records</td><td>Check facts, discuss patterns privately and apply policy consistently.</td></tr><tr><td>Teamwork</td><td>Handover quality, cooperation and specific observed behaviours</td><td>Recognise helpful conduct and address behaviour gaps with examples.</td></tr><tr><td>Development</td><td>Demonstrated skills, completed practice and follow-up observations</td><td>Provide coaching, practice time and a clear review date.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Use evidence in context. A single bad result may reflect equipment failure, unrealistic staffing or an unusual demand spike rather than an individual's capability. Check the system as well as the person, and record information accurately without adding speculation.</p></section>
<section id="accountability"><h2>10. Accountability without blame</h2><p>Accountability means that commitments, decisions and results are visible and followed through. A blame culture encourages people to hide mistakes; a learning culture expects people to report issues promptly while still addressing repeated negligence or misconduct fairly.</p><ul><li>Make the expected outcome and deadline explicit.</li><li>Ask people to raise barriers early rather than conceal delays.</li><li>Review facts and contributing conditions after a miss.</li><li>Agree corrective action, owner and review date.</li><li>Close the loop and communicate the learning to the people who need it.</li></ul><p>For an incident or near miss, protect people first, follow the reporting procedure and preserve accurate facts. Do not discourage reporting, alter records or promise an outcome before an appropriate review.</p></section>
<section id="scenario-practice"><h2>11. Interactive leadership scenarios</h2><p>Choose a situation and your first response. Review the coaching guidance to reflect on the leadership principle involved.</p><label for="scenario">Select a scenario</label><select id="scenario"><option value="peak">Peak-period task overload</option><option value="repeat">Repeated missed handover detail</option><option value="conflict">Team conflict in front of customers</option><option value="newstarter">New employee unsure of a critical procedure</option></select><label for="response">Your first response</label><select id="response"><option value="a">Take over the work and deal with the team later</option><option value="b">Clarify immediate risk and priorities, assign ownership, then follow up</option><option value="c">Publicly identify who caused the problem</option><option value="d">Ignore it unless a customer complains</option></select><button type="button" id="review-scenario">Review my response</button><div class="output" id="scenario-output" aria-live="polite">Select a scenario and a response to see coaching guidance.</div></section>
<section id="knowledge-check"><h2>12. Knowledge check</h2><p>Choose one answer for each question, then check your score. Use missed answers as a prompt for review, not as a substitute for required workplace assessment.</p><form id="quiz">
<div class="question"><p><strong>1. What should a useful delegation instruction include?</strong></p><label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="a"> A vague task and an expectation that the employee will work it out</label><label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="b"> A clear outcome, standard, authority, resources and check-in</label><label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="c"> Only the deadline, regardless of risk or capability</label></div>
<div class="question"><p><strong>2. When giving corrective feedback, the supervisor should primarily focus on:</strong></p><label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="a"> Personal labels and assumptions about motivation</label><label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="b"> Comparing the employee with a favourite team member</label><label><input type="radio" name="q2" value="c"> Observable behaviour, the standard, impact and next action</label></div>
<div class="question"><p><strong>3. Two employees argue during a busy service. What is the best immediate priority?</strong></p><label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="a"> Stabilise the operation, protect people and assign immediate coverage</label><label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="b"> Conduct a full investigation in front of guests</label><label><input type="radio" name="q3" value="c"> Decide who is at fault before checking facts</label></div>
<div class="question"><p><strong>4. A new employee is unsure how to perform a safety-critical task. The supervisor should:</strong></p><label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="a"> Let the employee experiment without supervision</label><label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="b"> Ask another inexperienced employee to explain it</label><label><input type="radio" name="q4" value="c"> Stop unsafe independent work, demonstrate the approved method and verify competence</label></div>
<div class="question"><p><strong>5. What is the purpose of a shift briefing?</strong></p><label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="a"> Deliver a long speech with no questions</label><label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="b"> Align the team on demand, roles, priorities, risks and escalation</label><label><input type="radio" name="q5" value="c"> Replace all written procedures and handover records</label></div>
<div class="question"><p><strong>6. A performance target is missed once. What is a fair first step?</strong></p><label><input type="radio" name="q6" value="a"> Check evidence and context, then discuss the gap and contributing factors</label><label><input type="radio" name="q6" value="b"> Assume the employee deliberately failed</label><label><input type="radio" name="q6" value="c"> Publicly reprimand the whole team</label></div>
<button type="button" id="score-quiz">Check my answers</button><button type="reset" class="secondary">Reset quiz</button><div class="output" id="quiz-result" aria-live="polite">Your result will appear here.</div></form></section>
<section id="checklist"><h2>13. Supervisor development checklist</h2><p>Tick an item only when you can give a real example. Use the checklist for self-review or a coaching conversation.</p><div id="portfolio-checklist"><label><input type="checkbox"> I can lead a concise briefing and check team understanding.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I delegate with clear outcomes, authority, resources and follow-up.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I give feedback based on observed behaviour and agreed standards.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I can coach a team member through a skill or performance gap.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I respond calmly to conflict and know when to escalate.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I monitor results fairly and consider operational context.</label><label><input type="checkbox"> I record and close actions according to workplace policy.</label></div><label for="development-action">My next development action</label><textarea id="development-action" rows="3" placeholder="Example: Lead the next pre-shift briefing and ask my manager for feedback afterwards."></textarea><button type="button" id="save-checklist">Create development summary</button><div class="output" id="checklist-output" aria-live="polite">Your summary will appear here.</div><p class="muted">This page does not save information to a server. Copy or print your summary if you wish to retain it.</p></section>
<section id="next-steps"><h2>14. Put the learning into practice</h2><ol><li>Choose one upcoming shift and prepare a briefing using the planner.</li><li>Delegate one suitable task using a clear result, standard and check-in.</li><li>Observe one team behaviour and provide specific, respectful feedback.</li><li>Ask a manager or trusted colleague to review your approach.</li><li>Record what changed, what evidence you observed and what you will practise next.</li></ol><p>Frontline leadership improves through deliberate practice, consistent standards and honest reflection. Use your organisation's policies for safety, HR, attendance, performance and incident handling.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn primary" href="leadership-people-development-module-2.html">Review Module 2: Trainer Skills</a><a class="btn" href="leadership-people-development-academy.html">Return to Academy pathway</a></div></section>
